Subject: [PATCH] fonts: use OS/2 win metrics for ascent/descent under a Windows profile

A profile claiming Windows reports the wrong fontBoundingBox, hanging and
ideographic baselines, and CSS line boxes for every family whose OS/2 win
metrics differ from its hhea metrics. The shared Linux path in
AscentDescentWithHacks reads hhea-based SkFont metrics, while the
DirectWrite-backed Windows path reports usWinAscent/usWinDescent. Measured
at 40px against the pinned captures:

- Consolas: win 1884 -> 37 (reference) vs hhea 1521 -> 30 (ours)
- Calibri: win 1950 -> 38 (reference) vs hhea 1536 -> 30 (ours)
- Palatino Linotype: win 2150 -> 42 (reference) vs hhea 1499 -> 29 (ours)

Families whose tables agree (Arial, Tahoma, Verdana, Times New Roman)
already match, which confirms the files are right and only the selection
policy is wrong. The hanging baseline is 80% of the same ascent on both
sides, so it follows automatically, as do clientrects line boxes
(Consolas 24px: win 2398 units -> 28px vs hhea 2048 -> 24px).

When the profile claims Windows and no CSS ascent/descent override is set,
read the OS/2 and head table prefixes through the same getTableData path
the VDMX code in this function uses, and return the rounded win metrics.
DirectWrite never does bytecode hinting, so VDMX and the Linux borrow-one
adjustment are bypassed on this path, exactly as they are absent on
Windows. Missing tables, zero units, or zero win values fall through to
the existing path. Native builds are untouched: without a Windows profile
nothing is read and nothing changes.

Ledger: fonts.windows-win-metrics.
diff --git a/third_party/blink/renderer/platform/fonts/font_metrics.cc b/third_party/blink/renderer/platform/fonts/font_metrics.cc
--- a/third_party/blink/renderer/platform/fonts/font_metrics.cc
+++ b/third_party/blink/renderer/platform/fonts/font_metrics.cc
@@ -67,7 +67,45 @@ void FontMetrics::AscentDescentWithHacks(
   if (ascent_override)
     metrics.fAscent = -platform_data.size() * ascent_override.value();
   if (descent_override)
     metrics.fDescent = platform_data.size() * descent_override.value();

+  // A profile claiming Windows uses the OS/2 win metrics, which is what the
+  // DirectWrite-backed path reports on Windows. The shared Linux path below
+  // reads hhea-based SkFont metrics, so families whose win and hhea metrics
+  // differ (Consolas, Calibri, Palatino Linotype) report the wrong
+  // fontBoundingBox, hanging/ideographic baselines and CSS line boxes.
+  // With no Windows profile, or when CSS ascent/descent overrides are set,
+  // or when the tables are absent, nothing is touched.
+  if (!ascent_override && !descent_override) {
+    bool use_windows_metrics = false;
+    if (const auto* profile = base::apostate::Profile::Get()) {
+      if (const auto& platform = profile->ua_platform()) {
+        use_windows_metrics = (*platform == "Windows");
+      }
+    }
+    if (use_windows_metrics) {
+      // usWinAscent/usWinDescent live at OS/2 offsets 74/76 in all table
+      // versions; unitsPerEm at head offset 18. Read only the prefixes.
+      static const uint32_t kOs2Tag = SkSetFourByteTag('O', 'S', '/', '2');
+      static const uint32_t kHeadTag = SkSetFourByteTag('h', 'e', 'a', 'd');
+      uint8_t os2[78];
+      uint8_t head[20];
+      if (face->getTableSize(kOs2Tag) >= sizeof(os2) &&
+          face->getTableSize(kHeadTag) >= sizeof(head) &&
+          face->getTableData(kOs2Tag, 0, sizeof(os2), os2) == sizeof(os2) &&
+          face->getTableData(kHeadTag, 0, sizeof(head), head) == sizeof(head)) {
+        const unsigned upm = (head[18] << 8) | head[19];
+        const unsigned win_ascent = (os2[74] << 8) | os2[75];
+        const unsigned win_descent = (os2[76] << 8) | os2[77];
+        if (upm != 0 && win_ascent != 0 && win_descent != 0) {
+          const float size = platform_data.size();
+          ascent = SkScalarRoundToScalar(win_ascent * size / upm);
+          descent = SkScalarRoundToScalar(win_descent * size / upm);
+          return;
+        }
+      }
+    }
+  }
+
   int vdmx_ascent = 0, vdmx_descent = 0;
   bool is_vdmx_valid = false;
